todo

refactor the codebase:

Here's what I found after a thorough scan of the codebase:



    Refactoring Opportunities

    1. Giant spec files in pqo-core/src/ (highest impact)

    Problem: Four files are all enormous and follow an identical pattern:

    | File        | Lines | Issue                                                                                            |
    |-------------|-------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    | organism.rs | 3484  | hello_organism_builder is a single function with hundreds of repetitive stream/value definitions |
    | emergent.rs | 931   | Same pattern                                                                                     |
    | crystal.rs  | 853   | Same pattern                                                                                     |
    | worm.rs     | 428   | Same pattern (smaller but still repetitive)                                                      |

    Each has:
    - Nearly identical helper functions (ss, sr, srw, kernel, etc.) — copy-pasted across files
    - include_str!("../../../kernels/...") for Metal kernels
    - Config struct + builder function pattern
    - Repetitive .stream(dynamic_stream(...)) chains with hardcoded arrays like for name in committed { ... }

    Opportunity: Extract a shared spec_builder crate or module with:
    - Common helpers (ss, sr, srw, kernel, dynamic_stream, scalar_stream, etc.)
    - A SpecSpec trait or builder pattern to eliminate the repetition
    - Move packaged_metal_implementation to a shared location

    2. pqo-core/src/builder.rs (1705 lines)

    Problem: ModuleBuilder is huge but mostly a fluent builder with 10+ collection fields. The ModuleGraph has 10+ graph types
    (KernelGraph, PassGraph, ScheduleGraph, etc.) that all have identical Deref<[T]> + IntoIterator boilerplate (see model.rs:64-123).

    Opportunity:
    - Extract a Graph<T> generic wrapper that handles Deref + IntoIterator once instead of repeating it 3+ times
    - Consider whether the fluent builder needs 10 separate Vec fields — could be Vec<GraphItem> with an enum, or a more generic collection

    3. pqo-core/src/model.rs (732 lines)

    Problem: One file with 20+ types — ModuleGraph, ResourceGraph, KernelGraph, PassGraph, ScheduleGraph, DataType, ScalarType,
    Unit, Literal, ValueNode, StreamNode, KernelNode, SlotNode, KernelAbi, PassNode, Binding, ViewNode, ScheduleNode,
    BackendImplementation, etc.

    Opportunity: Split by domain:
    - model/types.rs — DataType, ScalarType, Unit, Literal
    - model/graph.rs — ModuleGraph, ResourceGraph, KernelGraph, PassGraph, ScheduleGraph
    - model/nodes.rs — ValueNode, StreamNode, KernelNode, SlotNode, PassNode, ViewNode, ScheduleNode
    - model/abi.rs — KernelAbi, DispatchIndex, ThreadgroupBehavior, AliasingRule, BackendImplementation, Backend

    4. pqo-validator/src/lib.rs (2674 lines)

    Problem: 12 validation passes all in one file. Each pass is a distinct concern (structural refs, types, resource access, capacity,
    bindings, hazards, schedule DAG, buffer versions, backend ABI, observation points, determinism, fingerprint).

    Opportunity: Split into modules:

    pqo-validator/
      src/
        lib.rs          — entry, Validator struct, orchestration
        structural.rs   — pass 1
        types.rs        — pass 2
        access.rs       — pass 3
        capacity.rs     — pass 4
        bindings.rs     — pass 5
        hazards.rs      — pass 6
        schedule.rs     — pass 7
        buffers.rs      — pass 8
        abi.rs          — pass 9
        observations.rs — pass 10
        determinism.rs  — pass 11
        fingerprint.rs  — pass 12


    5. pqo-syntax/src/lib.rs (1852 lines)

    Problem: Lexer, parser, and lowerer all in one file. The lex function alone is hundreds of lines with manual byte-level parsing.

    Opportunity: Split into:
    - lexer.rs — tokenization
    - parser.rs — AST construction
    - lower.rs — lowering to ModuleGraph

    6. pqo-ui-panel/src/main.rs (1666 lines)

    Problem: Tauri app with chat DB management, OpenAI agent integration, keychain access, hot-reload, GPU telemetry, window management, and
    IPC all in one file.

    Opportunity: Split by concern:
    - agent.rs — OpenAI agent calls, chat management
    - db.rs — chat JSON persistence
    - keychain.rs — keyring operations
    - reload.rs — hot-reload logic
    - gpu_telemetry.rs — GPU metrics
    - windows.rs — windowing integration

    7. Repetitive constant definitions

    Problem: Across organism.rs, crystal.rs, emergent.rs, worm.rs — each defines its own CONFIG struct with a reference()
    constructor that does the same thing. Each defines its own ss/sr/srw/kernel helpers.

    Opportunity: A shared SpecConfig trait and SpecBuilder to eliminate ~400+ lines of duplicated helper functions.



    Priority ranking

    1. pqo-validator split — biggest file, cleanest boundaries (12 distinct passes)
    2. pqo-core model.rs split — 732 lines of mixed concerns, clean domain boundaries
    3. pqo-core spec file dedup — organism/emergent/crystal/worm share ~400 lines of duplicated helpers
    4. pqo-syntax split — lexer/parser/lowerer are naturally separate
    5. pqo-ui-panel split — large but more risky (Tauri interop)
    6. pqo-core builder.rs — big but the current structure works; lower urgency
    7. Graph<T> generic — nice-to-have, eliminates boilerplate but lower impact

    Want me to write a detailed plan for any of these?